.TH COMB 1 "2026" "comb" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
comb \- view, filter and copy log files in a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comb
.RB [ \-e
.IR REGEX ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B comb
loads a log file (or piped stdin) into a scrollable pane with severity
highlighting, service-tag coloring, an incremental filter, line marking and
clipboard copy. When stdin is a pipe it is read automatically; a live file is
followed as it grows, surviving truncation and rotation.
.PP
Filters match literal text by default with smart case (a pattern containing
no uppercase letters matches case-insensitively). A leading
.B ^
or trailing
.B $
anchors to the line start or end.
Ctrl\-R in the filter prompt switches to POSIX ERE mode; the status bar shows
.B (R)
while regex mode is on. Ctrl\-V excludes matching lines instead of showing
them, like
.BR "grep \-v" ;
the status bar shows
.B (!)
while active.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " REGEX"
start with
.I REGEX
as the initial (regex-mode) filter
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-color ", " \-C
disable syntax coloring; also honors the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable. Cursor and marks remain inverse video.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
show help and exit
.TP
.B \-
read from stdin; implied when stdin is not a tty
.TP
.B \-\-
treat all following arguments as positional, not options
.SH KEYS
The key bindings below are factory defaults compiled into the binary;
there is currently no remapping configuration. The filter prompt accepts
editing keys; Up/Down/PgUp/PgDn stay live there so
results can be scrolled while typing (terminal wheel scroll arrives as these
keys too).
.TP
.BR j ", " e ", " Enter ", " Down
next line
.TP
.BR k ", " Up
previous line
.TP
.BR d ", " Ctrl\-d ", " PgDn ", " Ctrl\-f
page down
.TP
.BR u ", " Ctrl\-u ", " PgUp ", " b
page up
.TP
.BR g ", " Home
go to top
.TP
.BR G ", " End
go to bottom
.TP
.BR h ", " Left ", " l ", " Right
scroll sideways by 8 columns; 0 or ^ go to the left edge, $ follows the
current line's end
.TP
.B /
open filter prompt (incremental)
.TP
.B ?
clear the filter
.TP
.B Esc
close the prompt; otherwise clear marks if any, else clear the filter
.TP
.BR Space ", " x
mark / unmark the current line and sweep in the direction of the last
vertical move
.TP
.BR c ", " y
copy marked lines (and clear the marks), else the current line. The
OSC 52 escape sequence is always emitted, so copy works over ssh and in
supporting terminals; helper programs (wl\-copy, xclip, pbcopy,
termux\-clipboard\-set) are additionally spawned when available
.TP
.B f
toggle follow mode
.TP
.B w
toggle soft line wrap
.TP
.B r
reload the file from disk
.TP
.B q ", " Ctrl\-c
quit
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
disable syntax coloring when set
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY
gate the clipboard helper search: wl\-copy is only tried when
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set, xclip only when
.B DISPLAY
is. With both set (common under XWayland) the Wayland helper wins because
it is tried first; pbcopy and termux\-clipboard\-set are session\-independent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR less (1)
